  "globalNotes": "US episodes are the Yardeni Research / S&P Dow Jones Indices monthly table 'S&P 500 Bull & Bear Markets Since 1928' (published 2024-01-21; bears = declines of 20%+ on closing prices; months derived as calendar difference of the sourced start/end months). Derived aggregates (computed from that table, rounded to 1 decimal): 22 bears since 1929 averaged 11.2 months (median 8.5) with an average decline of -36.6%; 24 completed bulls averaged 34.8 months with an average gain of +121.4%. Post-WWII subset (1946-2022): 13 bears averaged 12.0 months and -32.9%; 12 completed bulls averaged 62.5 months, i.e. bulls run roughly 3-5x longer than bears. Other published averages for calibration: Morningstar (1926-2014, S&P 500): bear ~13 months / ~-30%, bull ~8.5 years / ~+458%; Hartford Funds/Natixis: bear ~289 days (~9.6 months), bull ~988 days (~33 months) with ~+112% average bull gain. Typical bear duration is thus ~9-18 months; definitions (close vs intraday, 20% threshold) shift counts materially. Japan is the key outlier: Nikkei 225 closed at 38,915.87 on 1989-12-29, fell ~-82% to an intraday low of 6,994.90 on 2008-10-28 (nearly 19 years after the peak; interim 2003 low ~7,600, approx), and did not reclaim its 1989 peak until 2024-02-22 - secular bear regimes abroad can persist 10+ years, far beyond any US episode (the longest US bear, 1930-1932, lasted 26 months). Emerging-market crisis bears tend to be shorter and sharper than US bears: in the 1997-98 Asian crisis the Thai stock market dropped ~75% and Hong Kong's Hang Seng fell 23% in four days (Oct 1997), with EM crisis drawdowns commonly on the order of -50% to -80% within 1-2 years (approx); Russia's MOEX fell -43.6% in four trading days in Feb 2022. Post-source events (approx, not in the Yardeni table): the bull that began 2022-10 was still open as of the source date; the 2025 'tariff crash' drawdown (~-19% close-basis, ~-23% intraday at the 2025-04 trough, per Wikipedia) stayed just short of the -20% closing-basis bear threshold, and the Nikkei set records in 2026.",
